---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Env from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Env.astro";
import Sidenote from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Sidenote.astro";
import M from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/M.astro";
import Code from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Code.astro";
import Stability from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Stability.astro";
import Figure from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Figure.astro";
import PostHeader from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/PostHeader.astro";
import CellSketch from "../../components/CellSketch.astro";
import Tex from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Tex.astro";
import KatexAssets from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/KatexAssets.astro";
import { u } from "../../data/nav";
---
<Docs title="Long-form writing" status="experimental" lede="Notes, explainers, and blog posts: the post header, sidenotes, margin figures, footnotes, theorem environments, and collapsible detail.">
  <KatexAssets slot="head" />
  <h2>Post header</h2>
  <PostHeader level={3} title="Example note: the Hodge Laplacian, drawn" lede="A worked example on one triangle." date="2026-09-14" minutes={6} tags={["Topology"]} />
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<PostHeader
  title="Example note: the Hodge Laplacian, drawn"
  lede="A worked example on one triangle."
  date="2026-09-14"
  minutes={6}
  tags={["Topology"]}
/>`} />
  <p>The title is set like a paper's, so a site's posts and papers read as one series. The date prints in full, as "14 September 2026"; pass <code>locale</code> for another language. In an <code>&lt;Article&gt;</code>, give it <code>slot="header"</code> and it lines up with the text. Its title sizes to the header's own width, so the header takes its width from where it sits; in a box that shrinks to fit its content, give it a width. The <a href={u("/patterns/post/")}>blog post pattern</a> shows the whole page.</p>

  <h3 id="entry">An entry without a date</h3>
  <p>A project page or a section has no date. Leave out <code>date</code>, give the facts for the meta line in <code>meta</code>, and put actions in the default slot, under the tags. <code>stop</code> ends the title in the tomato full stop for a page that is a section.</p>
  <PostHeader level={3} title="Example project" lede="A plotting library for simplicial data." meta={["Open source", "2025"]} tags={["Python"]}>
    <p><a class="ply-button ply-button--sm" href="#entry">Source</a></p>
  </PostHeader>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<PostHeader title="Example project" lede="..." meta={["Open source", "2025"]} tags={["Python"]}>
  <p><a class="ply-button ply-button--sm" href="https://github.com/...">Source</a></p>
</PostHeader>`} />

  <h2 id="tex">Math in text from data</h2>
  <p><code>M</code> sets one formula in markup. A summary, an abstract, or a caption that lives in front matter or a JSON file has no markup to put it in, so <code>Tex</code> takes the whole text: each <code>$...$</code> renders through KaTeX at build time, <code>*italic*</code>, <code>**bold**</code>, and <code>***both***</code> become <code>em</code> and <code>strong</code>, and the rest is written out as it is. Each formula stays on one line, since a short formula split after its operator reads wrong. <code>html</code> passes trusted inline HTML through, for a caption.</p>
  <p><Tex text={String.raw`A $k$-simplex has $\binom{n}{k+1}$ faces in an $n$-complex, so a pass over **all** cells costs *one* sweep.`} /></p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<p><Tex text={paper.tldr} /></p>
<p><Tex text={String.raw\`Sums over $n \times d$ cells, in *one* pass.\`} /></p>`} />
  <p><code>Tex</code> does not load KaTeX's stylesheet. <code>M</code> and <code>Equation</code> import it as a linked stylesheet, which holds the first paint until it arrives. A page that cares about its first paint puts <code>KatexAssets</code> in its head instead, when <code>hasMath</code> says its text has some. That writes the stylesheet inline and preloads the two faces nearly every formula uses, Main-Regular and Math-Italic. The rarer faces load when a formula draws them; <code>preload="all"</code> preloads all five. This page does so. Write a literal dollar sign as <code>\$</code>, and in a YAML front matter field put text with backslashes in single quotes.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`import { KatexAssets, Tex, hasMath } from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro";

<head>{hasMath(paper.tldr, paper.abstract) && <KatexAssets />}</head>`} />
  <p><code>emphasis(text)</code> and <code>plain(text)</code> from the same module turn the markers into markup or drop them, for a search description or a feed.</p>

  <h2>Theorem environments</h2>
  <Env kind="definition" num="2.1" name="Simplicial complex">
    A simplicial complex <M tex={String.raw`K`} /> on a vertex set <M tex={String.raw`V`} /> is a set of nonempty finite subsets of <M tex={String.raw`V`} /> such that every nonempty subset of a member is also a member.
  </Env>
  <Env kind="theorem" num="2.2">
    The kernel of the Hodge Laplacian <M tex={String.raw`L_k`} /> is isomorphic to the <M tex={String.raw`k`} />-th homology group of <M tex={String.raw`K`} /> over the reals.
  </Env>
  <Env kind="proof">
    The kernel of <M tex={String.raw`L_k`} /> is the intersection of the kernels of <M tex={String.raw`B_k`} /> and <M tex={String.raw`B_{k+1}^{\top}`} />, which are the harmonic <M tex={String.raw`k`} />-chains, and these represent homology classes one to one.
  </Env>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Env kind="definition" num="2.1" name="Simplicial complex">...</Env>
<Env kind="theorem" num="2.2">...</Env>
<Env kind="proof">...</Env>`} />
  <p>Kinds: definition, theorem, lemma, proposition, corollary, remark, example, proof. Theorems and lemmas set their body in italics, as print does; definitions sit on the wall gray; proofs end with a tomato box.</p>

  <h2>Sidenotes</h2>
  <p>A sidenote keeps a remark next to the sentence it belongs to.<Sidenote n={1}>When the page has room after the text, the note sits there, in the margin. On a narrow screen it drops below the line.</Sidenote> Use them for asides a curious reader wants and a hurried one can skip.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<p>A sentence.<Sidenote n={1}>The aside.</Sidenote> More text.</p>`} />

  <h2>In the margin</h2>
  <Figure margin num={1}>
    <CellSketch />
    <Fragment slot="caption">A 2-cell and its three oriented edges.</Fragment>
  </Figure>
  <p>A small figure, table, or code block can sit in the same margin as the sidenotes, beside the paragraph after it. Place it before that paragraph and give it <code>.ply-margin</code>, or pass <code>margin</code> to <code>&lt;Figure&gt;</code>. Where the margin is closed, it stays in the text, above that paragraph.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Figure margin num={1}>
  <img src={sketch.src} alt="A filled triangle with oriented edges." />
  <Fragment slot="caption">A 2-cell and its three oriented edges.</Fragment>
</Figure>
<p>The paragraph it sits beside.</p>`} />
  <p>Keep margin blocks small: 14rem wide is room for a sketch, a short table, or a few lines of code. Anything larger belongs in the <a href={u("/foundations/layout/#articles")}>text</a>.</p>

  <h2>Collapsible detail</h2>
  <details class="ply-details">
    <summary>Derivation of the update rule</summary>
    <p>Long derivations, extra tables, and setup details go here, closed by default, so the main argument reads straight through.</p>
  </details>
  <Code lang="html" code={`<details class="ply-details">
  <summary>Derivation of the update rule</summary>
  <p>...</p>
</details>`} />

  <h2>Emphasis</h2>
  <p>Inside <code>.ply-prose</code>, emphasis is set in type alone. <em>Italic</em> is the true italic face, one step heavier than the body so it reads as a change of voice. <strong>Bold</strong> stays bold, and <strong><em>both together</em></strong> are bold italic. Use italic for a term being introduced or a word the sentence stresses, and bold for what a reader must not miss.</p>
  <Code lang="html" code={`<p>A <em>sheaf</em> assigns data to open sets. <strong>Read this first.</strong></p>`} />

  <h2>Quotes</h2>
  <blockquote class="ply-quote">
    Quotes keep a thin rule and a larger size, and the source goes underneath.
    <cite>Source, year</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Footnotes</h2>
  <p>For references that should not interrupt the line, use a numbered list in <code>.ply-footnotes</code> at the end of the article.</p>
</Docs>
